t portions of the finished works."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R-6e8e14d6-bcfb-44b3-a4b8-554d7a2b037b","fromVersionId":"4fdb6746-50c7-4c39-bde6-cbd86b247923","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R-a5895ddf-0728-4328-a9c3-063bdce6363f","problem_id":"cbfc105a-3d9a-49a4-b2d1-28dd1731e077","from_version_id":"6547f5d6-66a8-415b-986e-ea649aae5373","expect":{"passage":"Botanists studying the desert plant genus Fouquieria have documented an unusual survival strategy: the shrub sheds its leaves entirely during prolonged droughts and regrows them within days of a single rainfall. This rapid cycling, which can occur several times in a single year, ______ allows the plant to photosynthesize whenever moisture is briefly available while minimizing water loss during dry stretches."},"set":{"passage":"Botanists studying the desert plant genus Fouquieria have documented an unusual survival strategy: the shrub sheds its leaves entirely during prolonged droughts and regrows them within days of a single rainfall. This rapid cycling, which can occur several times in a single year, ______ the plant to photosynthesize whenever moisture is briefly available while minimizing water loss during dry stretches."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R-a5895ddf-0728-4328-a9c3-063bdce6363f","fromVersionId":"6547f5d6-66a8-415b-986e-ea649aae5373","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R-b4cdabde-f747-4dc9-80d3-c5565cbb3bac","problem_id":"7249eebb-92c0-478b-bfbb-e4f067eb881e","from_version_id":"b8527c88-062e-48c1-915d-654959f9eac2","expect":{"passage":"Art historians studying the Dutch Golden Age have generally attributed the rapid rise of still-life painting in the seventeenth century to the growing wealth of merchant-class patrons who wanted decorative, secular art for their homes rather than religious commissions for churches. This economic explanation accounts for the sheer volume of still-life production, but it struggles to explain a more specific puzzle: why so many of these paintings incorporated hidden symbols of mortality, such as wilting flowers, half-peeled fruit, or extinguished candles, tucked among otherwise celebratory displays of abundance. A closer look at sermons and moral literature circulating in Dutch cities during this period reveals that vanitas themes, warning against the vanity of earthly pleasures, were a dominant strain of public religious discourse even as church commissions declined. Because this moral discourse continued to shape how ordinary citizens thought about wealth and mortality, painters and patrons alike remained conscious of the tension between material display and spiritual warning. ______, the paintings may reflect not simply new patrons' tastes but a broader cultural anxiety about material wealth that persisted independently of formal religious institutions."},"set":{"passage":"Art historians studying the Dutch Golden Age have generally attributed the rapid rise of still-life painting in the seventeenth century to the growing wealth of merchant-class patrons who wanted decorative, secular art for their homes rather than religious commissions for churches. This economic explanation accounts for the sheer volume of still-life production, but it struggles to explain a more specific puzzle: why so many of these paintings incorporated hidden symbols of mortality, such as wilting flowers, half-peeled fruit, or extinguished candles, tucked among otherwise celebratory displays of abundance. A closer look at sermons and moral literature circulating in Dutch cities during this period reveals that vanitas themes, warning against the vanity of earthly pleasures, were a dominant strain of public religious discourse even as church commissions declined. Because this moral discourse continued to shape how ordinary citizens thought about wealth and mortality, painters and patrons alike remained conscious of the tension between material display and spiritual warning. ______ the paintings may reflect not simply new patrons' tastes but a broader cultural anxiety about material wealth that persisted independently of formal religious institutions."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R-b4cdabde-f747-4dc9-80d3-c5565cbb3bac","fromVersionId":"b8527c88-062e-48c1-915d-654959f9eac2","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R-w1-boundaries-m-042","problem_id":"b94c1469-5fa9-4ea1-a5f4-fba1da245127","from_version_id":"d9f3e116-c5f3-4b2b-9050-154cc85a08ad","expect":{"passage":"In the early 1950s, psychologist Solomon Asch asked participants to match the length of a reference line to one of three comparison lines, a task so simple that people tested alone almost never erred. When seated among confederates who unanimously gave a clearly wrong answer, however, participants went along with the group on roughly a third of the critical trials______ some later reported that they had begun to doubt their own eyesight, while others admitted they had simply wanted to avoid standing out."},"set":{"passage":"In the early 1950s, psychologist Solomon Asch asked participants to match the length of a reference line to one of three comparison lines, a task so simple that people tested alone almost never erred. When seated among confederates who unanimously gave a clearly wrong answer, however, participants went along with the group on roughly a third of the critical trials______ later reported that they had begun to doubt their own eyesight, while others admitted they had simply wanted to avoid standing out."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R-w1-boundaries-m-042","fromVersionId":"d9f3e116-c5f3-4b2b-9050-154cc85a08ad","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R-w1-boundaries-m-254","problem_id":"23f1c9ff-e1da-42dd-8b26-ab7d7fa84d99","from_version_id":"4619a753-019f-4f91-98cf-1298403ad0c1","expect":{"passage":"Wabi-sabi, an aesthetic that finds beauty in imperfection and impermanence, shaped how Japanese potters approached their craft. Raku tea bowls were often left with uneven rims and irregular glazes by their makers, among them the sixteenth-century potter Chōjirō, whose rough, hand-formed bowls were prized by the tea master Sen no Rikyū______ such flaws were not regarded as mistakes but as deliberate invitations to contemplate the passage of time."},"set":{"passage":"Wabi-sabi, an aesthetic that finds beauty in imperfection and impermanence, shaped how Japanese potters approached their craft. Raku tea bowls were often left with uneven rims and irregular glazes by their makers, among them the sixteenth-century potter Chōjirō, whose rough, hand-formed bowls were prized by the tea master Sen no Rikyū______ flaws were not regarded as mistakes but as deliberate invitations to contemplate the passage of time."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R-w1-boundaries-m-254","fromVersionId":"4619a753-019f-4f91-98cf-1298403ad0c1","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R-w1-boundaries-m-259","problem_id":"3fa06af6-a564-4909-ab48-53816e374822","from_version_id":"cdb4b64f-6824-49a2-80c5-aba3d7b66c5d","expect":{"passage":"Persian classical music is organized not into fixed scales but into the radif, a body of melodic models grouped into modal systems called dastgahs. Several of these systems depend on intervals smaller than a Western semitone, most famously the koron, a pitch lowered by roughly a quarter of a whole step______ Iranian composer Alireza Mashayekhi has drawn on such intervals in works that fuse traditional modes with avant-garde techniques."},"set":{"passage":"Persian classical music is organized not into fixed scales but into the radif, a body of melodic models grouped into modal systems called dastgahs. Several of these systems depend on intervals smaller than a Western semitone, most famously the koron, a pitch lowered by roughly a quarter of a whole step______ composer Alireza Mashayekhi has drawn on such intervals in works that fuse traditional modes with avant-garde techniques."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R-w1-boundaries-m-259","fromVersionId":"cdb4b64f-6824-49a2-80c5-aba3d7b66c5d","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R-rebal13:w3-boundaries-m-038","problem_id":"be1276fa-bb0b-4ccb-bb24-3a3d49a55e31","from_version_id":"8f7e0950-4576-4935-83ef-f9d8a00fde6e","expect":{"passage":"From his seat beside the tram window, Senhor Oliveira had watched the Saints' procession wind down Rua da Graça for fifty-one Junes. This year, a spark from a paper lantern caught the hem of a small boy's Saint Anthony costume, and the crowd, a tangle of grandmothers, fishmongers, and brass-band players who had been singing together only moments earlier, ______ neighbors shoved past neighbors, and no one seemed certain whose child the boy was."},"set":{"passage":"From his seat beside the tram window, Senhor Oliveira had watched the Saints' procession wind down Rua da Graça for fifty-one Junes. This year, a spark from a paper lantern caught the hem of a small boy's Saint Anthony costume, and the crowd, a tangle of grandmothers, fishmongers, and brass-band players who had been singing together only moments earlier, ______ shoved past neighbors, and no one seemed certain whose child the boy was."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R-rebal13:w3-boundaries-m-038","fromVersionId":"8f7e0950-4576-4935-83ef-f9d8a00fde6e","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"D-18ec5fbb","problem_id":"a3676b4b-3fe9-4210-8ef5-91d32cbf7ac0","from_version_id":"ff0aef8f-2af3-477a-be2d-4cd728c3b7d3","expect":{"passage":"A marine biology research team is tracking the migration speed of a pod of gray whales along the Pacific coast. The team recorded the whales' position at several checkpoints during a single continuous swim, measuring distance traveled (in nautical miles) and elapsed time (in hours) from the start of tracking, shown in the scatterplot below. The line of best fit is also shown.\n\nThe team wants to convert the whales' average speed, based on the line of best fit, into miles per day, using the fact that 1 nautical mile = 1.15 miles.\n\nBased on the line of best fit, what is the whales' average speed, in miles per day, rounded to the nearest whole number?"},"set":{"passage":"A marine biology research team is tracking the migration speed of a pod of gray whales along the Pacific coast. The team recorded the whales' position at several checkpoints during a single continuous swim, measuring distance traveled (in nautical miles) and elapsed time (in hours) from the start of tracking, shown in the scatterplot below. The line of best fit is also shown.\n\nThe team wants to convert the whales' average speed, based on the line of best fit, into miles per day, using the fact that 1 nautical mile = 1.15 miles."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[],"alt":"산점도(점 6개), 가로축 Elapsed time (hours), 세로축 Distance traveled (nautical miles), 추세선 y = 4x + 1."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"D-18ec5fbb","fromVersionId":"ff0aef8f-2af3-477a-be2d-4cd728c3b7d3","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"D-5b9b865b","problem_id":"683eb1d2-b4b0-47d3-a6fa-ceb69df37411","from_version_id":"bf10e141-cdb0-4017-b9f6-ebd05f8c2144","expect":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- Barter systems, in which goods and services are exchanged directly without money, were common in many early societies.\n- Barter requires a \"double coincidence of wants\": each party must want what the other offers.\n- Metal coins emerged in several ancient civilizations, including Lydia around the 7th century BCE, partly to solve this matching problem.\n- Coins allowed value to be standardized and stored, making trade over long distances easier.\n- Paper money later developed in China during the Tang and Song dynasties as a lighter alternative to carrying metal coins.\n- Today, most transactions occur through digital records rather than physical currency.\n\nThe student wants to explain why metal coins were introduced as an advance over barter. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"},"set":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- Barter systems, in which goods and services are exchanged directly without money, were common in many early societies.\n- Barter requires a \"double coincidence of wants\": each party must want what the other offers.\n- Metal coins emerged in several ancient civilizations, including Lydia around the 7th century BCE, partly to solve this matching problem.\n- Coins allowed value to be standardized and stored, making trade over long distances easier.\n- Paper money later developed in China during the Tang and Song dynasties as a lighter alternative to carrying metal coins.\n- Today, most transactions occur through digital records rather than physical currency."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.554Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"D-5b9b865b","fromVersionId":"bf10e141-cdb0-4017-b9f6-ebd05f8c2144","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"D-fcde57e1","problem_id":"c1af76c6-c1a7-4192-bc4c-ae685c5a45d0","from_version_id":"6ce50e0c-737c-464a-98b0-f180d1859635","expect":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- The saguaro cactus is native to the Sonoran Desert in Arizona, California, and northern Mexico.\n- Saguaros grow very slowly, sometimes taking up to 75 years to develop their first side arm.\n- Some saguaros live for more than 150 years and can grow taller than 40 feet.\n- The flower of the saguaro cactus is the official state flower of Arizona.\n- Saguaro flowers bloom at night and close by the following afternoon, making them accessible to nocturnal pollinators like bats.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize how long it takes for a saguaro cactus to reach physical maturity. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"},"set":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- The saguaro cactus is native to the Sonoran Desert in Arizona, California, and northern Mexico.\n- Saguaros grow very slowly, sometimes taking up to 75 years to develop their first side arm.\n- Some saguaros live for more than 150 years and can grow taller than 40 feet.\n- The flower of the saguaro cactus is the official state flower of Arizona.\n- Saguaro flowers bloom at night and close by the following afternoon, making them accessible to nocturnal pollinators like bats."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"D-fcde57e1","fromVersionId":"6ce50e0c-737c-464a-98b0-f180d1859635","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"D-3b4169b4","problem_id":"f42d1cb6-1801-4905-b44f-7395e22b10b6","from_version_id":"6972e94c-f336-486a-9e51-ef9e70b718c3","expect":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- In the 1960s, urban planners in several U.S. cities began replacing streetcar lines with bus routes, arguing that buses offered greater route flexibility.\n- Streetcars require fixed tracks and overhead wires, which are expensive to install and maintain but tend to have longer operational lifespans than buses.\n- A 2015 study found that cities with light rail systems, a modern successor to streetcars, saw a 15% increase in ridership within five years of construction compared to comparable bus-only corridors.\n- Buses can be rerouted quickly in response to construction, accidents, or changing demand, without the need for costly infrastructure changes.\n- Fixed rail lines tend to attract more private real estate investment near stations because developers view rail infrastructure as a permanent commitment to a corridor.\n- Bus rapid transit (BRT) systems attempt to combine rail-like dedicated lanes with the flexibility of buses, though they often lack the permanence signal that attracts investment.\n\nThe student wants to explain why real estate developers might prefer investing near rail lines rather than near bus routes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"},"set":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- In the 1960s, urban planners in several U.S. cities began replacing streetcar lines with bus routes, arguing that buses offered greater route flexibility.\n- Streetcars require fixed tracks and overhead wires, which are expensive to install and maintain but tend to have longer operational lifespans than buses.\n- A 2015 study found that cities with light rail systems, a modern successor to streetcars, saw a 15% increase in ridership within five years of construction compared to comparable bus-only corridors.\n- Buses can be rerouted quickly in response to construction, accidents, or changing demand, without the need for costly infrastructure changes.\n- Fixed rail lines tend to attract more private real estate investment near stations because developers view rail infrastructure as a permanent commitment to a corridor.\n- Bus rapid transit (BRT) systems attempt to combine rail-like dedicated lanes with the flexibility of buses, though they often lack the permanence signal that attracts investment."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"D-3b4169b4","fromVersionId":"6972e94c-f336-486a-9e51-ef9e70b718c3","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"D-896b1d19","problem_id":"b8c8a807-9aed-4d9b-a946-a4ab161e63b6","from_version_id":"8fdad52c-475b-41fd-860d-d32978feac07","expect":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor but support roughly 25% of all known marine species.\n- Rising ocean temperatures cause coral bleaching, a process in which corals expel the symbiotic algae that provide them with food and color.\n- Bleached corals are not immediately dead; if water temperatures return to normal quickly, corals can sometimes recover and reabsorb algae.\n- The Great Barrier Reef experienced mass bleaching events in 2016, 2017, and 2020, with each event affecting different sections of the reef.\n- Ocean acidification, caused by absorption of excess atmospheric carbon dioxide, makes it harder for corals to build their calcium carbonate skeletons.\n- Marine biologists are experimenting with selectively breeding heat-resistant coral strains to help reefs withstand future warming events.\n\nThe student wants to explain why coral bleaching does not always result in the death of a coral colony. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"},"set":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor but support roughly 25% of all known marine species.\n- Rising ocean temperatures cause coral bleaching, a process in which corals expel the symbiotic algae that provide them with food and color.\n- Bleached corals are not immediately dead; if water temperatures return to normal quickly, corals can sometimes recover and reabsorb algae.\n- The Great Barrier Reef experienced mass bleaching events in 2016, 2017, and 2020, with each event affecting different sections of the reef.\n- Ocean acidification, caused by absorption of excess atmospheric carbon dioxide, makes it harder for corals to build their calcium carbonate skeletons.\n- Marine biologists are experimenting with selectively breeding heat-resistant coral strains to help reefs withstand future warming events."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"D-896b1d19","fromVersionId":"8fdad52c-475b-41fd-860d-d32978feac07","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"D-eec841e6","problem_id":"699b7b32-ed03-4320-8d1b-fff4adbc3290","from_version_id":"cc25597e-b4f0-4851-aa6b-c494644005c1","expect":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- The Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer produced fewer than 40 known paintings during his lifetime, a small output compared to contemporaries like Rembrandt.\n- Vermeer often used a rare and expensive pigment called ultramarine, made from crushed lapis lazuli imported from Afghanistan, even in underpainting layers not visible in the finished work.\n- X-ray analysis of Vermeer's paintings has revealed that he frequently reworked compositions, painting over earlier versions of figures and objects.\n- Art historians believe Vermeer's small output may be partly explained by his meticulous, slow working method and his use of costly materials.\n- Vermeer worked in Delft, a city with a thriving art market, but he sold most of his paintings to a single wealthy patron rather than on the open market.\n- After Vermeer's death, his reputation faded for nearly two centuries until 19th-century critics rediscovered and championed his work.\n\nThe student wants to explain why Vermeer completed so few paintings during his career. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"},"set":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- The Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer produced fewer than 40 known paintings during his lifetime, a small output compared to contemporaries like Rembrandt.\n- Vermeer often used a rare and expensive pigment called ultramarine, made from crushed lapis lazuli imported from Afghanistan, even in underpainting layers not visible in the finished work.\n- X-ray analysis of Vermeer's paintings has revealed that he frequently reworked compositions, painting over earlier versions of figures and objects.\n- Art historians believe Vermeer's small output may be partly explained by his meticulous, slow working method and his use of costly materials.\n- Vermeer worked in Delft, a city with a thriving art market, but he sold most of his paintings to a single wealthy patron rather than on the open market.\n- After Vermeer's death, his reputation faded for nearly two centuries until 19th-century critics rediscovered and championed his work."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"D-eec841e6","fromVersionId":"cc25597e-b4f0-4851-aa6b-c494644005c1","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"D-ef704d02","problem_id":"2644ce3e-8cf5-4f08-be68-1cf00a8ea2a1","from_version_id":"51513a91-5812-4b05-961c-57728ca6bd43","expect":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- The Monarch butterfly (Danaus plexippus) migrates up to 3,000 miles each fall from Canada and the northern United States to overwintering sites in central Mexico.\n- Monarchs use a time-compensated sun compass, adjusting their flight direction based on the sun's position and the time of day.\n- Individual monarchs live only 2 to 6 weeks during the breeding season, so no single butterfly completes the entire round-trip migration.\n- The generation that migrates south in fall, called the \"super generation,\" can live up to 8 months, long enough to fly to Mexico and begin the journey back north.\n- Scientists have found that monarchs also use the Earth's magnetic field as a backup navigational cue on overcast days when the sun is not visible.\n- Habitat loss along migration corridors, especially the decline of milkweed plants, threatens the monarch's ability to complete its migration.\n\nThe student wants to explain how monarch butterflies are able to navigate accurately even when the sun is obscured by clouds. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"},"set":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- The Monarch butterfly (Danaus plexippus) migrates up to 3,000 miles each fall from Canada and the northern United States to overwintering sites in central Mexico.\n- Monarchs use a time-compensated sun compass, adjusting their flight direction based on the sun's position and the time of day.\n- Individual monarchs live only 2 to 6 weeks during the breeding season, so no single butterfly completes the entire round-trip migration.\n- The generation that migrates south in fall, called the \"super generation,\" can live up to 8 months, long enough to fly to Mexico and begin the journey back north.\n- Scientists have found that monarchs also use the Earth's magnetic field as a backup navigational cue on overcast days when the sun is not visible.\n- Habitat loss along migration corridors, especially the decline of milkweed plants, threatens the monarch's ability to complete its migration."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"D-ef704d02","fromVersionId":"51513a91-5812-4b05-961c-57728ca6bd43","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"D-f6ad8197","problem_id":"9818fe8b-4aa5-4a51-bd2c-ee99310afb22","from_version_id":"8cc8b081-db7f-4516-9d12-dfc3dfafe2f6","expect":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- During the Tulip Mania of the 1630s in the Dutch Republic, prices for rare tulip bulbs rose to levels exceeding the cost of a house in Amsterdam.\n- Tulip bulbs were traded through futures contracts, allowing buyers to purchase bulbs that would not be delivered until the following growing season.\n- The tulip market was especially vulnerable because bulbs cannot be inspected for their eventual flower pattern until they actually bloom, making value highly speculative.\n- In February 1637, prices collapsed suddenly when buyers at a routine bulb auction refused to pay previously agreed prices, triggering panic selling.\n- Some economic historians argue that Tulip Mania's effects on the broader Dutch economy were smaller than popular accounts suggest, since trading was largely limited to wealthy merchants.\n- Tulip Mania is often cited as one of the earliest recorded speculative asset bubbles in economic history.\n\nThe student wants to explain why buyers were willing to pay extremely high prices for tulip bulbs whose actual quality could not yet be confirmed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"},"set":{"passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n- During the Tulip Mania of the 1630s in the Dutch Republic, prices for rare tulip bulbs rose to levels exceeding the cost of a house in Amsterdam.\n- Tulip bulbs were traded through futures contracts, allowing buyers to purchase bulbs that would not be delivered until the following growing season.\n- The tulip market was especially vulnerable because bulbs cannot be inspected for their eventual flower pattern until they actually bloom, making value highly speculative.\n- In February 1637, prices collapsed suddenly when buyers at a routine bulb auction refused to pay previously agreed prices, triggering panic selling.\n- Some economic historians argue that Tulip Mania's effects on the broader Dutch economy were smaller than popular accounts suggest, since trading was largely limited to wealthy merchants.\n- Tulip Mania is often cited as one of the earliest recorded speculative asset bubbles in economic history."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"D-f6ad8197","fromVersionId":"8cc8b081-db7f-4516-9d12-dfc3dfafe2f6","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"E-P06a","problem_id":"47056b8e-21e4-4e4c-ab4a-d4efb2276c16","from_version_id":"b679e222-ad35-4559-84f5-8e09e9aedba9","expect":{"explanation_en":"The text begins with Kalani's observation that Hedwig winds the clock backward, which he takes as mischief. Then a request to see the ledger follows, and it emerges that the dates she wrote herself are a week ahead of the real ones, so the action is reread as turning time back to shield her father. At the end, Hedwig looks at Kalani and he meets her gaze without a word, leaving it undecided whether he will stay silent, so the answer is A. B reverses the order of realization, C summarizes only the first half, and D misreads the open ending as a resolution of the debt."},"set":{"explanation_en":"The text begins with Kalani's observation that Hedwig winds the clock backward, which he takes as mischief. Then a request to see the ledger follows, and it emerges that the dates she wrote herself are a week earlier than the real ones, so the action is reread as turning time back to shield her father. At the end, Hedwig looks at Kalani and he meets her gaze without a word, leaving it undecided whether he will stay silent, so the answer is A. B reverses the order of realization, C summarizes only the first half, and D misreads the open ending as a resolution of the debt."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"E-P06a","fromVersionId":"b679e222-ad35-4559-84f5-8e09e9aedba9","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"E-P06b","problem_id":"457d567a-8ed3-4ca3-9e3d-989326420aa4","from_version_id":"78e523ad-d682-4f41-9d7e-b257d990b6ac","expect":{"explanation_en":"At first, Mathis reads the letters in an exaggerated theatrical voice, and the narrator applauds trivial complaints such as the price of leeks and the neighbor's dog. Then a letter appears saying their mother feared being trapped in Lyon, his voice drops, and the narrator thinks of the plane ticket of a brother who is ready to leave. Therefore, the answer is C: the tone shifts from playful amusement to a sober recognition tied to a departure. A's relief, B's resentment about packing, and D's agreement have no support in the text."},"set":{"explanation_en":"At first, Mathis reads the letters in an exaggerated theatrical voice, and the narrator applauds trivial complaints such as the price of leeks and the neighbor's dog. Then a letter appears saying their mother feared being trapped in Lyon, her voice drops, and the narrator thinks of Mathis's plane ticket, a sign that she is ready to leave. Therefore, the answer is C: the tone shifts from playful amusement to a sober recognition tied to a departure. A's relief, B's resentment about packing, and D's agreement have no support in the text."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"E-P06b","fromVersionId":"78e523ad-d682-4f41-9d7e-b257d990b6ac","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"E-P06c","problem_id":"d07ca7d3-eec2-4221-bdd6-7fbca6489315","from_version_id":"a3f5839d-ceda-49de-94b3-86760f55080f","expect":{"explanation_en":"Shanice is the last person to harvest salt by hand on the coast after her children left for the city, and she calls the narrator, her tenant, a nephew and teaches the craft every day. When illness keeps her from working, she hands over the rake and says this is the only lease she ever truly meant. Putting the two clues together, passing on the craft was the real promise between them, so the answer is A. B, C, and D are wrong because they read the rental contract and money literally."},"set":{"explanation_en":"Shanice is the last person to harvest salt by hand on the coast after her children left for the city, and she calls the narrator, her tenant, a niece and teaches the craft every day. When illness keeps her from working, she hands over the rake and says this is the only lease she ever truly meant. Putting the two clues together, passing on the craft was the real promise between them, so the answer is A. B, C, and D are wrong because they read the rental contract and money literally."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"E-P06c","fromVersionId":"a3f5839d-ceda-49de-94b3-86760f55080f","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"E-P06d","problem_id":"b4af5fd9-678f-4f8c-82f4-45905d194cad","from_version_id":"ffe513f9-3f56-4040-99d1-6ffbcc4fca9c","expect":{"passage":"For decades, marine biologists assumed that octopuses were solitary creatures with little tolerance for one another outside of mating. This view was based largely on observations of species in captivity, where octopuses placed together often fought or cannibalized one another. Recent fieldwork at a site off the coast of Australia nicknamed \"Octlantis,\" however, has complicated this picture. There, researchers documented dozens of octopuses living in close proximity, denning within a few body lengths of each other for extended periods, and engaging in repeated visual displays and physical contact that did not end in violence. Some of these interactions even resembled the kind of signaling seen in more overtly social species, such as changes in skin color and posture directed at approaching neighbors. Researchers acknowledge that the density of octopuses at Octlantis may simply reflect a shortage of suitable denning sites nearby, forcing individuals into proximity regardless of their social preferences. ______ , they maintain that many of the observed interactions, including the signaling displays, appear only when other octopuses are within sight, suggesting the behavior is a genuine response to social presence rather than a mere byproduct of crowding.","explanation_en":"The sentence before the blank acknowledges the researchers' alternative explanation (a possible rebuttal) that the crowding may simply be due to a shortage of oysters, and the sentence after the blank maintains their original claim that the signaling behavior is a social response despite this. This is a concession relationship, in which a claim continues despite the earlier acknowledgment, so 'Even so,' is the most logical. 'For example,' signals an example relationship, 'Similarly,' signals an additional relationship through similarity, and 'Consequently,' signals a cause-and-effect relationship, so all are inappropriate.","explanation":"빈칸 앞 문장은 밀집 현상이 단지 굴 부족 때문일 수 있다는 대안적 설명(반박 가능성)을 연구자들이 인정하는 내용이고, 빈칸 뒤 문장은 그럼에도 불구하고 신호 행동이 사회적 반응이라는 자신들의 원래 주장을 유지하는 내용이다. 이는 앞선 인정에도 불구하고 주장을 이어가는 양보(concession) 관계이므로 'Even so,'가 가장 논리적이다. 'For example,'은 예시 관계, 'Similarly,'는 유사성을 통한 추가 관계, 'Consequently,'는 인과 관계를 신호하므로 모두 부적절하다."},"set":{"passage":"For decades, marine biologists assumed that octopuses were solitary creatures with little tolerance for one another outside of mating. This view was based largely on observations of species in captivity, where octopuses placed together often fought or cannibalized one another. Recent fieldwork at a site off the coast of Australia nicknamed \"Octlantis,\" however, has complicated this picture. There, researchers documented dozens of octopuses living in close proximity, denning within a few body lengths of each other for extended periods, and engaging in repeated visual displays and physical contact that did not end in violence. Some of these interactions even resembled the kind of signaling seen in more overtly social species, such as changes in skin color and posture directed at approaching neighbors. Researchers acknowledge that the density of octopuses at Octlantis may simply reflect a shortage of suitable denning sites nearby, forcing individuals into proximity regardless of their social preferences. ______ they maintain that many of the observed interactions, including the signaling displays, appear only when other octopuses are within sight, suggesting the behavior is a genuine response to social presence rather than a mere byproduct of crowding.","explanation_en":"The sentence before the blank acknowledges the researchers' alternative explanation (a possible rebuttal) that the crowding may simply be due to a shortage of suitable denning sites, and the sentence after the blank maintains their original claim that the signaling behavior is a social response despite this. This is a concession relationship, in which a claim continues despite the earlier acknowledgment, so 'Even so,' is the most logical. 'For example,' signals an example relationship, 'Similarly,' signals an additional relationship through similarity, and 'Consequently,' signals a cause-and-effect relationship, so all are inappropriate.","explanation":"빈칸 앞 문장은 밀집 현상이 단지 적합한 은신처(굴)가 부족하기 때문일 수 있다는 대안적 설명(반박 가능성)을 연구자들이 인정하는 내용이고, 빈칸 뒤 문장은 그럼에도 불구하고 신호 행동이 사회적 반응이라는 자신들의 원래 주장을 유지하는 내용이다. 이는 앞선 인정에도 불구하고 주장을 이어가는 양보(concession) 관계이므로 'Even so,'가 가장 논리적이다. 'For example,'은 예시 관계, 'Similarly,'는 유사성을 통한 추가 관계, 'Consequently,'는 인과 관계를 신호하므로 모두 부적절하다."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"E-P06d","fromVersionId":"ffe513f9-3f56-4040-99d1-6ffbcc4fca9c","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"E-M22","problem_id":"515a7e74-99e6-4afd-bd7a-809ac05b58db","from_version_id":"4a3c2460-6c52-4f70-95f0-a37a8409ac6c","expect":{"explanation_en":"Let b be the fixed fee and r the charge per mile. From the first condition, a 10-mile trip costs 8.50 dollars, so b + 10r = 8.50. When the rate doubles, the fare for 4 miles is b + 4(2r) = b + 8r = 7.30. Subtracting the two equations gives (b + 10r) − (b + 8r) = 8.50 − 7.30, that is, 2r = 1.20, so r = 0.60. Substituting this into the first equation gives b + 10(0.60) = 8.50, so b = 8.50 − 6.00 = 2.50. Therefore the fixed fee is 2.50 dollars and the answer is C.\n\nAnalysis of wrong answers:\n- 1.50: A value that could result from not solving the two equations together and reflecting only part of the second condition (the doubled rate), for example finding r = 0.60 and then mistakenly subtracting 4r (=2.40) instead of 10r from 8.50 (8.50-2.40 is 6.10, not 1.50, so this arises through a calculation error), leading to a wrong per-mile rate.\n- 2.10: If the student ignores the doubled-rate condition and wrongly sets the second equation as b+4r=7.30, then solving with the first equation gives 6r=1.20, r=0.20, and b=8.50-2.00=6.50 rather than 2.10; this is an approximate wrong answer that could arise from a sign mistake, such as subtracting in the opposite order.\n- 3.10: The student considers only the difference in distance (6 miles) from the 10-mile and 4-mile conditions, does not reflect the doubled rate, and wrongly finds the per-mile rate as (8.50-7.30)/(10-4)=0.20, giving b=8.50-10(0.20)=6.50 rather than 3.10; it is a value that could arise similarly from using only partial information.","explanation":"고정요금을 b, 마일당 요금을 r이라 하자. 첫 조건에서 10마일 이동 시 요금이 8.50달러이므로 b + 10r = 8.50이다. 요율이 2배가 되면 4마일 요금은 b + 4(2r) = b + 8r = 7.30이다. 두 식을 변끼리 빼면 (b + 10r) − (b + 8r) = 8.50 − 7.30, 즉 2r = 1.20이므로 r = 0.60이다. 이를 첫 식에 대입하면 b + 10(0.60) = 8.50이므로 b = 8.50 − 6.00 = 2.50이다. 따라서 고정 요금은 2.50달러이고 정답은 C번이다.\n\n오답 분석:\n- 1.50: r=0.60을 구한 뒤 10r 대신 잘못하여 4r(=2.40)을 8.50에서 뺀 값(8.50-2.40=6.10이 아니라 계산 실수로 1.50이 나오는 경우)처럼, 두 식을 연립하지 않고 두 번째 조건(요율 2배)의 의미를 일부만 반영해 마일당 요금을 잘못 구한 경우 나올 수 있는 값이다.\n- 2.10: 요율이 2배가 된다는 조건을 무시하고 두 번째 식을 b+4r=7.30으로 잘못 세운 뒤 첫 식과 연립하면 6r=1.20, r=0.20이 되고 b=8.50-2.00=6.50이 아니라, 식을 반대로 빼는 등 부호 실수로 얻어질 수 있는 근사 오답이다.\n- 3.10: 10마일과 4마일 조건에서 거리 차(6마일)만 고려하고 요율이 2배라는 조건을 반영하지 않은 채 단순히 (8.50-7.30)/(10-4)=0.20을 마일당 요금으로 잘못 구해 b=8.50-10(0.20)=6.50이 아니라, 유사한 방식으로 부분 정보만 사용했을 때 나올 수 있는 값이다."},"set":{"explanation_en":"Let b be the fixed fee and r the charge per mile. From the first condition, a 10-mile trip costs 8.50 dollars, so b + 10r = 8.50. When the rate doubles, the fare for 4 miles is b + 4(2r) = b + 8r = 7.30. Subtracting the two equations gives (b + 10r) − (b + 8r) = 8.50 − 7.30, that is, 2r = 1.20, so r = 0.60. Substituting this into the first equation gives b + 10(0.60) = 8.50, so b = 8.50 − 6.00 = 2.50. Therefore the fixed fee is 2.50 dollars and the answer is C.\n\nWhy the other choices fail: check each value against both conditions. If b = 1.50, then r = (8.50 - 1.50)/10 = 0.70 and b + 8r = 7.10, not 7.30. If b = 2.10, then r = 0.64 and b + 8r = 7.22, not 7.30. If b = 3.10, then r = 0.54 and b + 8r = 7.42, not 7.30. Only b = 2.50 satisfies both conditions (r = 0.60 and b + 8r = 7.30).","explanation":"고정요금을 b, 마일당 요금을 r이라 하자. 첫 조건에서 10마일 이동 시 요금이 8.50달러이므로 b + 10r = 8.50이다. 요율이 2배가 되면 4마일 요금은 b + 4(2r) = b + 8r = 7.30이다. 두 식을 변끼리 빼면 (b + 10r) − (b + 8r) = 8.50 − 7.30, 즉 2r = 1.20이므로 r = 0.60이다. 이를 첫 식에 대입하면 b + 10(0.60) = 8.50이므로 b = 8.50 − 6.00 = 2.50이다. 따라서 고정 요금은 2.50달러이고 정답은 C번이다.\n\n오답 확인: 각 값을 두 조건에 모두 대입해 본다. b = 1.50이면 r = (8.50 - 1.50)/10 = 0.70이고 b + 8r = 7.10으로 7.30이 아니다. b = 2.10이면 r = 0.64이고 b + 8r = 7.22로 7.30이 아니다. b = 3.10이면 r = 0.54이고 b + 8r = 7.42로 7.30이 아니다. 두 조건을 모두 만족하는 값은 b = 2.50(r = 0.60, b + 8r = 7.30)뿐이다."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"E-M22","fromVersionId":"4a3c2460-6c52-4f70-95f0-a37a8409ac6c","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"E-M28a","problem_id":"6df1d05b-4d1a-4905-860d-3cd5ad67b271","from_version_id":"04a3d390-7882-432e-8296-cddc0419905f","expect":{"explanation_en":"\"less than\" means adding (or subtracting) 5 relative to the base value 4x. So \"5 less than 4 times x\" translates to 4x - 5, which equals 23. That gives 4x - 5 = 23."},"set":{"explanation_en":"\"less than\" means subtracting 5 from the base value 4x. So \"5 less than 4 times x\" translates to 4x - 5, which equals 23. That gives 4x - 5 = 23."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"E-M28a","fromVersionId":"04a3d390-7882-432e-8296-cddc0419905f","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"E-M28b","problem_id":"cbf756d3-7be4-473f-8a70-343541c6d8fe","from_version_id":"77650779-89bc-448b-accf-0b00d97fbdd0","expect":{"explanation_en":"\"less than\" means adding (or subtracting) 2 relative to the base value 4x. So \"2 less than 4 times x\" translates to 4x - 2, which equals 34. That gives 4x - 2 = 34."},"set":{"explanation_en":"\"less than\" means subtracting 2 from the base value 4x. So \"2 less than 4 times x\" translates to 4x - 2, which equals 34. That gives 4x - 2 = 34."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"E-M28b","fromVersionId":"77650779-89bc-448b-accf-0b00d97fbdd0","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"E-M26","problem_id":"aea66ace-7029-4d01-90dc-79b86e1797c9","from_version_id":"a95c2467-57e9-4a61-981f-f7af43316241","expect":{"passage":"The amount of a radioactive substance, in grams, after t days is modeled by $A(t) = 70000 \\cdot 1.15^{t}$, where t ≥ 0."},"set":{"passage":"The mass of a bacteria culture, in grams, after t days is modeled by $A(t) = 70000 \\cdot 1.15^{t}$, where t ≥ 0."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"E-M26","fromVersionId":"a95c2467-57e9-4a61-981f-f7af43316241","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"SCAN-R-d69b18fc","problem_id":"d69b18fc-ecea-4f73-8aad-3a4589245ad4","from_version_id":"6493d3f3-2ab3-4bfe-acd2-f72faf124b4e","expect":{"passage":"In her 1983 book The Managed Heart, sociologist Arlie Russell Hochschild examined how flight attendants were trained to produce warmth on demand, smiling through turbulence and rude passengers alike. Her central concept, emotional labor, describes the effort workers expend to display the feelings that their employers ______ the hidden cost of this effort, Hochschild argued, is often a growing sense of estrangement from one's own emotions."},"set":{"passage":"In her 1983 book The Managed Heart, sociologist Arlie Russell Hochschild examined how flight attendants were trained to produce warmth on demand, smiling through turbulence and rude passengers alike. Her central concept, emotional labor, describes the effort workers expend to display the feelings that their employers ______ hidden cost of this effort, Hochschild argued, is often a growing sense of estrangement from one's own emotions."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.555Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"SCAN-R-d69b18fc","fromVersionId":"6493d3f3-2ab3-4bfe-acd2-f72faf124b4e","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"SCAN-R-784a7174","problem_id":"784a7174-0f18-4c7a-ac4d-3615e79e19cb","from_version_id":"737d2370-5a46-4fdb-8331-a9b6a84b9806","expect":{"passage":"Taiwo read the letter twice beneath the awning while the noon heat pressed down on Balogun Market. The solicitor's words were crisp and generous: the letter promised her a berth on a steamer bound for Liverpool and a room in her late uncle's London house______ it said nothing about who would sit each morning behind her mother's indigo stall, folding cloth the way her mother had always assumed Taiwo one day would."},"set":{"passage":"Taiwo read the letter twice beneath the awning while the noon heat pressed down on Balogun Market. The solicitor's words were crisp and generous: the letter promised her a berth on a steamer bound for Liverpool and a room in her late uncle's London house______ said nothing about who would sit each morning behind her mother's indigo stall, folding cloth the way her mother had always assumed Taiwo one day would."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.556Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"SCAN-R-784a7174","fromVersionId":"737d2370-5a46-4fdb-8331-a9b6a84b9806","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"AP-f02-k5","problem_id":"cecede20-5818-424e-90b6-80e2a40a0f6b","from_version_id":"c3d4c474-4887-4302-ac1c-5d2e3546a848","expect":{"passage":null},"set":{"passage":"f(t) is the cumulative net savings in dollars on a household energy bill t weeks after a smart thermostat is installed; f is differentiable on [0,10]. Given: $f(0) = 40$."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.556Z","issues":[],"alt":"Graph of f'. Horizontal axis: t (weeks), 0 to 10. Vertical axis: f'(t) (dollars per week), -3 to 7."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"AP-f02-k5","fromVersionId":"c3d4c474-4887-4302-ac1c-5d2e3546a848","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"run1:ap_calculus_ab-f02-k5","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"AP-f02-k9","problem_id":"1554274a-a01d-4025-8c4e-20fe01289129","from_version_id":"10d259a3-2d00-497d-90dc-fa4cec9159b9","expect":{"passage":null},"set":{"passage":"S(0)=0 dollars; S is differentiable on [0,10] and S' is the graph shown."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.556Z","issues":[],"alt":"Graph of S'. Horizontal axis: t (years since installation), 0 to 10. Vertical axis: S'(t) (dollars per year), -250 to 350."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"AP-f02-k9","fromVersionId":"10d259a3-2d00-497d-90dc-fa4cec9159b9","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"run1:ap_calculus_ab-f02-k9","item_index":0}},{"patch_id":"AP-f02-k10","problem_id":"95291c5b-4250-49c2-8c28-df0871aa7722","from_version_id":"c0f925d5-1285-4576-be3f-11accbc94fa2","expect":{"passage":null},"set":{"passage":"f is continuous on [0,6], differentiable on (0,6), and f(0)=1. f' is the derivative of f."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-13T15:54:00.556Z","issues":[],"alt":"Graph of y=f'(x). Horizontal axis: x, 0 to 6. Vertical axis: f'(x), -3 to 5."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"AP-f02-k10","fromVersionId":"c0f925d5-1285-4576-be3f-11accbc94fa2","fromVersionNo":1},"ap":{"candidate_key":"run1:ap_calculus_ab-f02-k10","item_index":0}}],"sets":[{"set_group_id":"57a95d4b-628c-44c6-9da2-172b6eeeea20","set_id":"b0935d08-1a6e-416d-a9ba-dcf9906d5dc0","name":"AP Calculus AB — Practice Exam","version_no":3,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["cecede20-5818-424e-90b6-80e2a40a0f6b"],"patch_ids":["AP-f02-k5"]},{"set_group_id":"0e792906-39d8-47e4-9826-afb2de63cb1a","set_id":"3b457ba7-620f-4f87-ba0c-30017a6b47e1","name":"AP Calculus AB — Practice Exam 2","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["1554274a-a01d-4025-8c4e-20fe01289129"],"patch_ids":["AP-f02-k9"]},{"set_group_id":"b29d0ad3-af3c-400d-86df-1c3700c817e6","set_id":"11ec8569-1d20-4f73-b7a2-74f217b57217","name":"AP Calculus BC — Practice Exam 1","version_no":2,"format":"ap_fixed","problem_ids":["95291c5b-4250-49c2-8c28-df0871aa7722"],"patch_ids":["AP-f02-k10"]},{"set_group_id":"693168bd-3fb0-4cdf-8dc4-21186e80cf39","set_id":"be21bac3-1235-4aa2-a396-a13f072b5b64","name":"SAT Practice Test 1","version_no":2,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["0b94e63d-1d30-4fdb-ad23-2ea8895e0978","20a54859-dce7-4280-9786-f449